import
	../../html

intro-kid html[]()
	- section "Syntax",
		- p quote
			Kid (Keen Interface for Data) is a serialization format like JSON.
			{br}
			It is equivalent to JSON and differs only in syntax.
		- p quote
			Kid looks exactly like Keen.
			It is just limited to data expressions with no variables, control flow, etc.
		- p "Valid Kid expressions are:"
		- bullets
			- quote
				{null}, booleans, numbers and strings, using the exact same syntax as JSON.
				({parens} also returns {null}.)
			- "Comma-separated or multi-line {fn-new} expressions, corresponding to JSON arrays."
			- "Named-new expressions, corresponding to JSON objects."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parse

			main void()
				kid-string = quote
					null: ()
					bool: false
					number: 1
					string: "hello"
					array: 1, 2
					another-array:
						- 1
						- 2
					object: x: 1, y: 2
					another-object:
						x: 1
						y: 2
				value json =
					null: ()
					bool: false
					number: 1
					string: "hello"
					array: 1, 2
					another-array:
						- 1
						- 2
					object: x: 1, y: 2
					another-object:
						x: 1
						y: 2
				parsed = parse kid, kid-string
				log value == parsed
				log value

		- aside-small quote
			{quote_} starts a multi-line string literal. More in {intro-link strings}.
			{br}
			In {function "parse"}{sp}{function "kid"}{comma}{variable "kid-string"},
			{function "kid"} is the name of a {std-type "keen/parse", "grammar"}.

		- p quote
			The {json} type is the type of a parsed JSON value.
			{br}
			Since Kid has the same data model as JSON, a parsed Kid value is also of type {json}.

		- aside-small quote
			{fn-log} logs its argument(s) as Kid.
			{br}
			So the above program's output looks like its source code, just normalized (such as sorting keys).

	- section "Keen and Kid",
		- p quote
			In the above example, {variable "value"} is created using a variety of functions (such as {fn-named-new})
			that return {json}.
			{br}
			Conveniently, the syntax is exactly the same as Kid.

		- p quote
			This is also true for typed objects.
			Below, the syntax to construct a {type "point"} in Keen is the exactly the same same
			as the syntax for the Kid expression that converts to it.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parse

			main void()
				kid-string = quote
					x: 1
					y: 2
				value point =
					x: 1
					y: 2
				parsed = to::point parse kid, kid-string
				log value == parsed
				log parsed

			point record(x float, y float)
			to point(a json)
				a["x"].to, a["y"].to

		- p "Since the syntax is the same, you can cut and paste data between Keen and Kid files."
		- p "It's also useful to be able to output a value in a format that is valid source code."

	- section "Empty values",
		- p quote
			Keen has a single syntax for empty values: {parens}.
			{br}
			JSON has 3: {null}, {code "[]"}, and {code "\{\}"}.
			{br}
			Usually you should just use {parens} (which means {null})
			and expect the user to treat it the same as other empty values.
			{br}
			In cases where it does matter, use {function "empty-array"} or {function "empty-object"}.
			This works the same in both Keen and Kid.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parse

			main void()
				kid-string = quote
					null: ()
					array: empty-array
					object: empty-object
				value json =
					null: ()
					array: empty-array
					object: empty-object
				parsed = parse kid, kid-string
				log value == parsed
				log parsed

	- section "Using JSON values",
		- p quote
			{std-function "keen/json", "subscript"} gets a value from a {json} value.
			If it is not an object containing the key, this returns {null} (displayed as {parens}).
		- p quote
			There are many functions named {fn-to} that convert {json} to various types.
			{br}
			These typically throw an exception if the {json} value does not look like what is expected.
			(Though as mentioned above, {null} is typically allowed as an empty array or object.)
			{br}
			See {std-module "keen/json"} to see them.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				j json =
					n: 1
					s: "ok"
				log j["n"]
				log to::nat j["n"]
				log to::symbol j["s"]
				log j["missing-key"]
				log to::symbol j["n"] # throws
